WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.489
Speaker 1: . There's a really interesting
piece I saw yesterday morning in the Hill

00:00:03.489 --> 00:00:08.540
written by Alexander Bolton that's called
Republican Worry's Republican Party Worries

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:13.190
Fiscal Conservatism is losing its rallying
cry.

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:15.809
And the article says a few interesting things.

00:00:15.809 --> 00:00:24.029
The big picture of which is the fiscal conservatism
that for so long was a big piece of the Republican

00:00:24.029 --> 00:00:33.460
Party mantra or ideology or policy framework
may be losing its sort of power and strength

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:37.270
as a unifying factor for the Republican Party.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:43.829
And Bolten writes instead, culture war issues
like immigration, religious freedom, LGBTQ

00:00:43.829 --> 00:00:50.140
rights, big tech and Black Lives Matter are
taking center stage in conservative politics,

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:54.940
saying that, quote, The initial shift in Republican
political priorities away from belt tightening

00:00:54.940 --> 00:01:00.120
coincided with the demise of the Tea Party,
the dominant force in the 2010 2010 midterm

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:04.370
elections, and the rise of now former President
Trump, who presided over an eight trillion

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:06.490
dollar increase in the national debt.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:07.930
I agree this is happening.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.170
I think that it actually started far earlier.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:20.100
In a sense, Reagan, while very fiscal conservatism
focused, started the path of the shift to

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.960
the cultural and social issues.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:28.369
Reagan did it by starting to talk about the
welfare queen with the Cadillac and all the

00:01:28.369 --> 00:01:31.119
kids just to make some food stamp money.

00:01:31.119 --> 00:01:35.399
And that led to Newt Gingrich, who pushed
the Republican Party more in the direction

00:01:35.399 --> 00:01:41.109
of the culture and social issues, which then
led to George W. Bush, who was essentially

00:01:41.109 --> 00:01:45.999
incompetent economically and from a foreign
policy standpoint, but was big on he's against

00:01:45.999 --> 00:01:49.320
abortion and he's born again and all this
different stuff.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.780
And then we got to Donald Trump.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:56.070
So so while I completely agree that this happened,
I think the timeline is actually a much more

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:59.159
nuanced and longer timeline.

00:01:59.159 --> 00:02:02.340
But needless to say, the analysis is correct.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:08.539
Alexander Bolton goes on to say, now Republicans
are having a tough time generating the same

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:13.620
outrage over Biden's multitrillion dollar
spending agenda as they did over former President

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.959
Obama's signature initiatives, the American
Relief and Reinvestment Act and the Affordable

00:02:17.959 --> 00:02:22.670
Care Act, which cost far less than what Biden
is proposing on infrastructure.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:24.950
I think that there's another aspect to that.

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:32.170
I don't think that it's because of Republican
hypocrisy that now they aren't that opposed

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:36.159
or actually are in favor of Joe Biden's one
point nine dollars, one point nine trillion

00:02:36.159 --> 00:02:41.870
dollar stimulus, or Joe Biden's planned infrastructure
spending, which also has some significant

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:42.960
Republican support.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:48.440
It's not that Republicans are saying, you
know, when Trump blew up the deficit, we were

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:49.480
OK with it.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.520
So maybe Joe Biden doing it isn't so bad.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.739
I don't think it's that at all.

00:02:53.739 --> 00:03:00.349
It's that the country is actually just all
united on those issues and always has been.

00:03:00.349 --> 00:03:06.760
And the political clout or influence that
the Republican Party had by criticizing the

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:10.340
spending is simply not having the same impression.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:12.499
I don't think it's the voters that have changed.

00:03:12.499 --> 00:03:18.650
I think it's that the Republican elected officials
have become less able to generate outrage

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:19.650
over it.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.900
We've seen no evidence that they actually
care about the hypocrisy.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:27.580
It's just that the Republican Party, given
everything that's going on, given how even

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:32.200
many in the Republican Party electorate now
understand that huge income inequality is

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:37.950
not so good, they understand that, you know,
deficit just for the sake of saying deficit

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:41.620
is bad is not something most of them are falling
for.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:48.999
I think that it's the electorate that has
actually come to realize that they've been

00:03:48.999 --> 00:03:53.859
fed best by their party, not that their views
have dramatically changed.

00:03:53.859 --> 00:04:00.329
And it used to be a sort of rallying cry that
really got people fired up.

00:04:00.329 --> 00:04:08.049
And now it's just not if you go back, Eisenhower
was the last Republican president to preside

00:04:08.049 --> 00:04:09.799
over a balanced budget.

00:04:09.799 --> 00:04:13.379
He balanced the budget in 1956 and 1957.

00:04:13.379 --> 00:04:19.509
And since then, it's been two Democrats that
balanced the budget, LBJ in 69 and Clinton

00:04:19.509 --> 00:04:26.759
during 1998 to 2001, followed by George W.
Bush taking over and exploding the deficit.

00:04:26.759 --> 00:04:32.490
So when you look at how the balanced budget
actually is far more.

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:38.090
And under Democratic presidents than Republicans,
you realize that the Republican Party's two

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:45.080
main economic policies cut taxes and spend
on the military don't actually count as fiscal

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:46.080
conservatism.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.270
They were never really about fiscal conservatism.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:51.550
They were about talking about fiscal conservatism.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:57.120
And then a basic, you know, finance 101, Econ
101, when you say, OK, the policy is you cut

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:03.210
taxes as much as possible, reduce revenue,
and then you spend more on the military, that

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:07.889
that's not actually fiscally conservative
and now they're not even pretending to be

00:05:07.889 --> 00:05:08.889
about that.

00:05:08.889 --> 00:05:14.849
So I agree very much with what Alexander Bolton
concludes here, which is the Republican Party

00:05:14.849 --> 00:05:20.849
is right to worry that fiscal conservatism
as a rallying cry and unifying sort of policy.

00:05:20.849 --> 00:05:23.810
It's not even really a policy idea.

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:24.810
It's a framework.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.949
It's a bullet point of some kind.

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:29.319
It's no longer working.

00:05:29.319 --> 00:05:35.500
And you have many Republicans approving of
Biden's stimulus plan, many Republicans approving

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.800
of Biden's proposed infrastructure plan.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:40.080
They're going to have to come up with something
else.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:43.539
They seem to be going in the direction of
making it these culture issues.

00:05:43.539 --> 00:05:48.379
The question is whether even that will work
because support for movements like Black Lives

00:05:48.379 --> 00:05:51.639
Matter is up even among the right.

00:05:51.639 --> 00:05:53.740
Not by that much, not by that much.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:59.410
But all of the hammering on BLM by the right
hasn't decreased support hasn't increased

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:01.120
much, but it hasn't decreased it.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.349
So I don't actually know what they need to
do to win the hearts and minds of voters,

00:06:06.349 --> 00:06:13.250
which explains perfectly why they are focusing
on suppressing the vote.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:16.150
They're realizing they're having trouble winning
any other way.

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:19.530
And so you suppress the vote, you try to win
in that way.

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:22.930
And that's exactly what they admitted they
were going to do back in 2012.

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:27.610
You go to the video of Mike Turzai, Pennsylvania,
saying voter ID is going to help Mitt Romney

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:28.610
win some states.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:31.129
That's not Mitt Romney is going to come up
with great ideas.

00:06:31.129 --> 00:06:35.710
It's not Mitt Romney understands what it is
that the average American wants.

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:37.780
Its voter ID will help Mitt Romney win.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:38.780
It didn't.

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:40.430
He lost badly to Barack Obama.

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:41.430
But this was all predictable.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:44.790
It's been predictable for a decade and it
is happening right now.

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:49.759
The question is, do they double down on the
voter suppression or do they start trying

00:06:49.759 --> 00:06:51.270
to win on policy?

00:06:51.270 --> 00:08:02.439
It seems that they're doing the voter suppression.

